# The `try...catch` Statement 🛡️

Enclose risky code inside a `try` block. If an error occurs, JavaScript halts execution inside `try` and immediately jumps to `catch`:

```javascript
try {
  const parsedData = JSON.parse("Invalid JSON string!");
  console.log("Parsed:", parsedData); // Never runs!
} catch (error) {
  console.error("Failed to parse JSON:", error.message);
}

console.log("Application continues running smoothly without crashing!");
```

